<section class="our-story">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Why This Exists</h2>
    <p>
      Growing online stores get offered two things: an agency that bills for deliverables, or a full-time
      e-commerce hire they can&rsquo;t justify yet. Neither one is a person who owns the store, knows what
      it did last quarter, and is around next month to answer for it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Infinite Frameworks is the third option. One senior operator, engaged directly, accountable for the
      storefront, the checkout, the catalog, and the systems underneath &mdash; and for what the numbers do
      after the work ships.
    </p>
    <p>
      No account manager between you and the person doing the work. No deliverable that ends at a
      handoff. The engagement is judged on revenue, margin, and how much of your week the store gives back.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="our-values">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>How the Work Runs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Measure first</strong>
        Nothing gets built before the baseline exists. If a change can&rsquo;t be judged against a number,
        it goes to the bottom of the list.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ship small</strong>
        Small, reversible changes with tracking already in place. Big-bang releases hide which decision was
        the wrong one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Own the seams</strong>
        Most e-commerce failures happen between systems, not inside them. The handoffs are the job, not
        somebody else&rsquo;s ticket.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Leave it documented</strong>
        Every decision, credential, and integration written down. You should be able to hand this to
        another operator and have them keep going.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Say when to stop</strong>
        If the next item isn&rsquo;t worth what it costs, I say so. Retainers that outlive their usefulness
        are how this work gets a bad name.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Plain reporting</strong>
        What shipped, what it moved, what it cost, what&rsquo;s next. Short enough that you actually read
        it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

